Z||000005||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-29-2001||11:15 PM||||You can replace the catalytic converter with a new version, they flow better.  <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cameron||04-30-2001||12:33 AM||||Congrats on joining the Fierohood!  May the Fiero Gods be kind to you during your stay.<P>On horsepower, aside from the other things mentioned, I'm not exactly positive, but the MSD 6A ignition works on 4-cylider cars and they are supposed to increase horsepower.  I'm thinking about one for my '87 GT.  But not positive on whether or not yet.<P>Good luck,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||04-30-2001||08:40 AM||||Better ignition = more horsepower?!<P>I may be uninformed on this, but it sounds like one of the old JC Whitney scam ads. If your ignition is sparking like its suppose to, and when it suppose to, putting a bigger spark in there is not gonna do much for you.<P> Horsepower is mostly a function of how much fuel and air mixture you can cram between the piston and the head, and how easily it escapes on the exhaust stroke. Ignition does not play a big part in that, unless the computer does not tell the plugs to spark at the right time.<P>   If you got the 4 cylinder motor, and you want a significant increase in power, then I would recommend you:<P>  1. start fixing up the rest of the car and save up your money for an engine transplant, keeping your eyes open for a good deal on a bigger motor<P>   or <P>  2. Get the car you have in best possible condition, and start looking around for a V6 Fiero, or one that someone has put a bigger motor into already, and when you find a good deal, grab it and sell this car to someone else.||||reg||1
Z||000008||1988LRC||04-30-2001||09:22 AM||||if you gotta beat that Eclipse there is no substitute for cubic inches||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||04-30-2001||10:26 AM||||Okay here is a little moral support for you. An '88 Forumla (best performace stock fiero made) sold on Easter morning on ebay in Baltimore for $1100. It had about 100k miles and looked liked it needed a valve cover gasket, a door hinge pin, and maybe seat covers.<P>There is one in Hudson, NY on ebay that looks like its gonna go in a few hours for about $2,000, that sounds like it is usable as is.<P>If you look around and check the lower exposure ad places (small town penny savers, bulletin boards, car parts stores for-sale cork boards) and let everyone that has anything to do with cars in your area know you want a V6 fiero, then you should be able to get a good deal on one.<P>  And your 4 clyinder auto, if you make it pretty, has lots of appeal for people that want a cute little car to drive around it, college girls, lonely housewives, first cars for teenage guys whose dads dont want to hand them a 200hp car...<P>  Its summer time. look around, keep your eyes open. You might find a good deal on some of the car web sites, but remember, the whole world sees those too, look for local stuff that has low exposure.<P>  If you really want performance get a V6 or better, and get the 5 speed manual transmission.<P>  And dont get down on your 2.5 auto either. Its not a fast car, but it does handle better than most things on the road, and it looks just the same as one with a V8 northstar conversion! Nobody knows whats under the hood unless you show them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||04-30-2001||12:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1988LRC:<BR><B>if you gotta beat that Eclipse there is no substitute for cubic inches</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Then why does a 600cc jap bike beat a 1340 Harly, not by a little bit, but flat out? Why does an Eclipse beat a Fiero w simular displacements. Why does my Fiero beat my 454 Chevy pu? That philosophy belongs on the drag strip. It may have been true (no substitution for cubic inches) twenty years ago when the best technology around was the hemispherical combustion chamber or dual overhead cams, but would cost an arm and a leg to buy. Technology was linear in application, so bigger was better. Now, Honda's VTECH sh!t is fast. Don't get me wrong I'm not a fan of jap-crap, but you can't live in denial that they produce more with less.||||reg||1
Z||000011||EdsB52||04-30-2001||12:17 PM||||I agree w/Ken. I don't know how you drive, but the 4cyl handles leagues above the 6, unless you do mods to the 6. I don't think the weight is that much more with the 6, but it's noticeable. Any puss can go fast in a straight line, but it takes nuts to go sideways through corners! Now I'll get flamed, oh well. If you're on the Utah Salt Flats about to lift-off at 300mph, or on a drag strip going 300 I can respect that, but to wind up a street car on a country road or deserted freeway to 140-150 mph isn't that exciting. I can't count the times I had my 4cyl Fiero jamming past pudstains (mustangs) through corners to have them blow by in the straights and have them punch their fist in the air in victory. You can take a rambler and make it compete with Corvettes if you have enough money. The moral is; buy what you want and be happy. You'll never reasonably get big HP from the Duke. Guys in here have put N02 to them and fryed 'em quick. I'd like to put a quad 4 in an 86-88 notchback w/a Getrag. I bet it would beat a stock 6cyl Fiero in all aspects.||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||04-30-2001||02:58 PM||||I'm sorry, no, you don't <B>have</B> to beat your friend's Eclipse.  You'll live.  In the end, you'll have a unique, cool-looking, fun sports car to boot.  Your friend will have, well, an Eclipse.<P>Face it, those E-clips are a dime a dozen.  If I were you, I'd just sit back, relax, let your friend rack up some tickets and repair bills while you enjoy your Fiero.  If you take care of it, you'll still be enjoying your plastic, midengine two-seater long after your bud's car is a worn out pile of rust.<P>And I really don't know of cheap and easy ways to increase horsepower.  Remember, you're not really just trying to increase horsepower, here, anyway - you're trying to improve performance.  They're not the same thing.<P>If you want your car to <B>perform</B>, start by giving it a thorough tune-up.  Do it yourself - don't pay someone else to do it.  Make sure the brakes aren't dragging, and that the tires are properly inflated - I mean air pressure, not the price  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Really.<P>When you get the car back to performing the way it was when it was new, go from there.  Thoroughly research any mods you might want to make (including here on the Forum), do them one at a time, and test the results as you go.  If at all possible, don't do any mod that you can't undo later, if you decide           it didn't pay off.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000013||Black88GT||04-30-2001||06:30 PM||||According to Ed Parks, the 2.5 weighs within 20 lbs. of the 2.8.  So I don't see how 20 lbs. is going to make that big of a difference.  I think it might be since most coupes often times had less options then GT's/SE's which made them lighter.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Galen||04-30-2001||07:25 PM||||First of all.. BLAH to ya'll v6 are better than i4 owners. You dont like i4's thats cool, but enough of the duke is puke junk. Atleast we can change our sparkplugs in less than 30 minutes(atleast I can). hehe<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000015||StuGood||04-30-2001||07:40 PM||||True - plugs are easier to change in a 4-cylinder, typically.  And you can get better gas mileage, too -!<P>I was going to say, "Once you drive the V6, you'll never go back."  But that would make me a liar - after my three V6 Fieros, what was the last one Stu bought?  A 1988 4-cylinder!||||reg||14
Z||000016||EdsB52||04-30-2001||08:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>According to Ed Parks, the 2.5 weighs within 20 lbs. of the 2.8.  So I don't see how 20 lbs. is going to make that big of a difference.  I think it might be since most coupes often times had less options then GT's/SE's which made them lighter.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I know what you mean, I heard that figure also. Do you agree or disagree that the 6cyl Fiero feels noticably heavier than the 4cyl when throwing it into a corner? Also, you have to account for the weight difference of the fastback body. My struts aren't new, but they seem solid.||||reg||1
